  "u": "blog/autocad-shortcuts-that-save-hours.html",
  "x": "AutoCAD Productivity Training Most people who feel \"slow\" in AutoCAD aren't missing skills. They're losing seconds on every single command, hunting through ribbons and menus. Those seconds add up to hours every week. Here are ten habits I teach in my free weekly webinar that make the biggest difference. 1. Type command aliases instead of clicking icons Almost every command has a short alias. Type it on the command line and press Enter or Space : L for Line, PL for Polyline, C for Circle, O for Offset, TR for Trim, EX for Extend and F for Fillet. Learn the ten you use most first. Your left hand stays on the keyboard and your right hand stays on the mouse. 2. Press Space (or Enter) to repeat the last command After finishing a command, pressing Space or Enter on an empty command line starts it again. Drawing ten circles? Type C once, then just press Space before each new one. 3. Use Space instead of Enter In most situations, Space works exactly like Enter (except when typing text). It's bigger and right under your thumb, which makes the alias-and-confirm rhythm much faster. 4. Open the Properties palette with Ctrl+1 Ctrl + 1 toggles the Properties palette. Select any object to change its layer, colour, linetype or"
