  "k": "article",
  "t": "SolidWorks vs Inventor vs Fusion 360: which should you learn?",
  "d": "An honest comparison of three popular 3D CAD tools from someone who uses all of them, and how to choose the right one for your career or project.",
  "u": "blog/solidworks-vs-inventor-vs-fusion-360.html",
  "x": "SolidWorks Inventor Fusion 360 Careers \"Which CAD software should I learn?\" is one of the questions I hear most in my training sessions. I use SolidWorks, Autodesk Inventor and Fusion 360 for client work, and the honest answer is that all three are excellent. The right one depends on what you want to do. Here's how they compare. SolidWorks SolidWorks, from Dassault Systèmes, is one of the most widely used mechanical CAD tools in industry. It's a Windows desktop application known for an approachable interface and strong part, assembly and drawing tools, with simulation available inside the same environment. A big advantage for job seekers is its certification path: CSWA (Associate), CSWP (Professional) and CSWE (Expert). A certification on your CV is clear proof of your skills. Best for: people aiming for product design and manufacturing roles, especially where job adverts ask for SolidWorks. Autodesk Inventor Inventor is Autodesk's professional mechanical design tool, also a Windows desktop application. Because it comes from the makers of AutoCAD, it works very smoothly with DWG drawings, which is a big plus in workplaces with years of AutoCAD files. It has powerful tools for sheet metal, frame structures and large asse"
