  "k": "article",
  "t": "What is Design for Manufacturing (DFM)? A practical guide",
  "d": "DFM explained simply: how designing with the workshop in mind cuts cost, avoids rework and gets parts made right the first time.",
  "u": "blog/what-is-design-for-manufacturing.html",
  "x": "DFM Sheet metal Engineering design A design can look perfect on screen and still be a nightmare to make. Design for Manufacturing (DFM) is the habit of designing parts with the workshop in mind from the very start, so they're quicker, cheaper and more reliable to produce. During my industrial attachment at KIRDI, I designed and fabricated jiko stove components from sheet metal. Applying DFM principles there made the difference between parts that assembled cleanly and parts that needed rework. Here are the ideas that matter most. Why DFM matters Most of a product's manufacturing cost is decided at the design stage, by the choice of material, the number of parts, the tolerances and the processes they require. Changing a CAD model is cheap. Changing a production run is not. 1. Reduce the number of parts Every extra part needs to be made, stored, handled and assembled. Ask whether two parts could become one, for example a bent sheet-metal bracket instead of two plates welded together. Fewer parts usually means fewer fasteners, fewer tolerances to stack up and faster assembly. 2. Design for the process you'll actually use A part made by laser cutting and bending has different rules from a part machined on a lathe or a mi"
